# env.example — template for runtime secrets (guide/06-secrets.md)
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   1. Copy this to a real, GIT-IGNORED file — e.g. ~/.env  (NOT in the repo), or a
#      repo-local  .env  that your .gitignore excludes (the templates already ignore **/.env).
#   2. Fill in real values.
#   3. Load it from your shell so tools/services see the vars. In ~/.zshenv (or ~/.bashrc):
#         [ -f "$HOME/.env" ] && set -a && . "$HOME/.env" && set +a
#      (`set -a` exports everything the file defines.)
#
# RULES (guide/06-secrets.md):
#   - This EXAMPLE (placeholders only) MAY be committed. The FILLED file NEVER is.
#   - Reference these vars in tracked config as ${VAR}, never the literal value.
#   - Prefer your OS keychain / a vault for high-value secrets; use a loaded env file
#     for convenience vars and things a service reads at startup.

# --- examples (replace + rename to what you actually use) ---
GITHUB_TOKEN=<your-github-token>            # a scoped PAT; more scope = more the operator can automate
OPENAI_API_KEY=<only-if-you-use-codex-with-an-api-key>
# SOME_SERVICE_TOKEN=<...>

# Non-secret but env-supplied config is fine here too, e.g.:
# EDITOR=vim
